{
  "format": "mercury-msgpack-hostile-header-vectors",
  "version": "1",
  "note": "Shared by the Java and Rust engines, byte-identical in both repositories. Every input is a top-level container, so it reaches a decoder through the same entry points as an event payload. A vector with expect=reject must fail as a decoding error - never as an unchecked exception, a panic, a stack overflow or an allocation sized by the hostile header - before anything is allocated for the declared count or length. A vector with expect=accept must decode to exactly the JSON in value (a JSON number without a fraction is an integer, any other a float64); the controls keep a decoder that refuses everything from passing. A control may carry canonical=reject: the event payload codec must decode it to value while the canonical package decoder must refuse it (empty input is the one case: the Java engine, the reference, reads it as an empty map and an empty envelope, and a package is never empty).",
  "vectors": [
    {
      "id": "array32-max-count-nothing-follows",
      "hex": "ddffffffff",
      "expect": "reject",
      "why": "count-exceeds-input",
      "description": "array 32 declaring 4,294,967,295 elements with no byte after the header"
    },
    {
      "id": "map32-count-whose-doubling-overflows-32-bits",
      "hex": "df80000000a16101a16201",
      "expect": "reject",
      "why": "count-exceeds-input",
      "description": "map 32 declaring 2,147,483,648 entries (the CVE-2026-90473 shape: 2N overflows a 32-bit int) with six bytes after the header"
    },
    {
